# Wired game controller -> stable symlinks /dev/joystick_wired and /dev/input/joystick_wired
#
# joy_linux (parameter dev:=/dev/input/joystick_wired) and the joystick_gui
# health check both expect /dev/input/joystick_wired. The kernel joydev name
# (/dev/input/jsN) is enumeration-order dependent, so a stable udev symlink is
# required.
#
# Detected on this machine (udevadm info -a -n /dev/input/js0):
#   ATTRS{idVendor}  == "045e"   (Microsoft)
#   ATTRS{idProduct} == "0b12"   (Xbox Series S|X Controller, wired / xpad)
#   ATTRS{name}      == "Microsoft Xbox Series S|X Controller"
#
# NOTE on the symlink target: SYMLINK+= is relative to /dev/, so to create
# /dev/input/joystick_wired you MUST write "input/joystick_wired" (not just
# "joystick_wired", which only makes /dev/joystick_wired). joy_linux and the
# GUI health check look under /dev/input/, so both entries are listed here.
# (Origin lesson: FITO_AMR_ros2_ws MANUAL_DRIVE.md, 2026-06-01 / 2026-06-11.)
#
# The original FITO platform used a DragonRise/Microntek PSX-clone (0079:0006);
# this machine's wired controller is the Xbox unit above, so the rule matches
# 045e:0b12 instead. If a different joystick is used, update idVendor/idProduct
# (re-run install_udev.sh with the device connected).
#
# Apply after editing:
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
# Verify:
#   ls -la /dev/input/joystick_wired
SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{idVendor}=="045e", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0b12", KERNEL=="js[0-9]*", SYMLINK+="joystick_wired input/joystick_wired", MODE="0666", GROUP="input"
